Let $\Phi$ be one of the varieties (6.2) and $X, Y \in \Phi$ such that $X<Y$ (i.e., $X \leqslant Y$ and $X 
eq Y$ ) and sig $X=\operatorname{sig} Y$. In particular, $X$ and $Y$ have the same rank, say $n$, and so $X, Y \in \Phi(n)$. In order to prove Theorem 6 it suffices to prove the following statement:\ (	extit{) There exists a $\Phi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ such that $Z \leqslant Y$.\ The proof of this statement will be given by induction on the rank $n$. The verification of (}) for small values of $n$ is straightforward and is omitted. The proof is divided into three sections: the next section treats the case $\Phi=\Pi$, the subsequent section the cases $\Phi=(\Pi, \Lambda)$ and ( $\Lambda, \Pi$ ), and the last one the cases $\Phi=(\Pi, 2 \Lambda)$ and $(2 \Lambda, \Pi)$.\ 15. The case $ oldsymbol{\Phi}=\Pi$. Before starting the proof we introduce some notations. To each $X \in \Pi$ we associate a $2 	imes \infty$ nonnegative integral matrix $\sigma(X)$ defined by  egin{equation*} \sigma(X)=\left( egin{array}{lll} a_{0} & a_{1} & a_{2} \cdots \ b_{0} & b_{1} & b_{2} \cdots \end{array}ight) 	ag{15.1} \end{equation*} where $\left(a_{k}, b_{k}ight)=\operatorname{sig} X^{(k)}$ for all $k$. It is easy to see that the entries of this matrix satisfy the following conditions:  egin{gather*} a_{0} \geqslant a_{1} \geqslant a_{2} \geqslant \ldots, \quad a_{k}=0 \quad 	ext { for large } k ; 	ag{15.2}\ b_{0} \geqslant b_{1} \geqslant b_{2} \geqslant \ldots, \quad b_{k}=0 \quad 	ext { for large } k ; 	ag{15.3}\ \quad a_{0} \geqslant b_{1} \geqslant a_{2} \geqslant b_{3} \geqslant \ldots ; 	ag{15.4}\ \quad b_{0} \geqslant a_{1} \geqslant b_{2} \geqslant a_{3} \geqslant \ldots ; 	ag{15.5}\ a_{0}-a_{1} \geqslant b_{1}-b_{2} \geqslant a_{2}-a_{3} \geqslant b_{3}-b_{4} \geqslant \ldots ; 	ag{15.6}\ b_{0}-b_{1} \geqslant a_{1}-a_{2} \geqslant b_{2}-b_{3} \geqslant a_{3}-a_{4} \geqslant \ldots . 	ag{15.7} \end{gather*} The diagram of $X$ can be recovered from $\sigma(X)$ as follows: The first column of the diagram consists of $a_{0}-a_{1}$ positive and $b_{0}-b_{1}$ negative nodes; the second column consists of $a_{1}-a_{2}$ positive and $b_{1}-b_{2}$ negative nodes, etc. Hence the map $\sigma$ is a bijection between $\Pi$ and the set of all $2 	imes \infty$ integral matrices whose entries satisfy the conditions (15.2)-(15.7). Let $X, Y \in \Pi(n)$ satisfy $X<Y$. We shall show that there exists a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ such that $Z \leqslant Y$. We use induction on $n$. For $n=0,1$ the assertion is vacuously true; so let $n>1$. Let $\sigma(X)$ be as above and let $$ \sigma(Y)=\left( egin{array}{lll} c_{0} & c_{1} & c_{2} \cdots \ d_{0} & d_{1} & d_{2} \cdots \end{array}ight) . $$ Since $X<Y$, we have  egin{equation*} a_{k} \leqslant c_{k}, \quad b_{k} \leqslant d_{k} \quad(k \geqslant 0) . 	ag{15.8} \end{equation*} Since $a_{0}+b_{0}=n=c_{0}+d_{0}$, it follows from (15.8) that $a_{0}=c_{0}$ and $b_{0}=d_{0}$.\ Assume that $X$ and $Y$ are not disjoint, i.e., there is a gene $g$ such that $X=X_{1}+g$ and $Y=Y_{1}+g$, with $X_{1}, Y_{1} \in \Pi$. Then $X_{1}<Y_{1}$ and by induction hypothesis there exists a $\Pi$-mutation $X_{1} ightarrow Z_{1}$ such that $Z_{1} \leqslant Y_{1}$. Then $X ightarrow Z=Z_{1}+g$ is also a $\Pi$-mutation and $Z \leqslant Y$. Thus we may assume (and we do) that $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint. Assume now that $\left(a_{k}, b_{k}ight)=\left(c_{k}, d_{k}ight) 
eq(0,0)$ for some $k \geqslant 1$. Since $X<Y$, we have $X^{(k)} \leqslant Y^{(k)}$. Since $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint, so are $X^{(k)}$ and $Y^{(k)}$ and consequently $X^{(k)}<Y^{(k)}$. By induction hypothesis there exists a $\Pi$-mutation $X^{(k)} ightarrow U$ such that $U \leqslant Y^{(k)}$. By the lifting property (Lemma 9) there exists a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ such that $Z^{(k)}=U$ and $Z \leqslant Y$. Hence from now on we may also assume that  egin{equation*} Y^{(k)} 
eq 0 \& k \geqslant 1 \Rightarrow a_{k}<c_{k} 	ext { or } b_{k}<d_{k} . 	ag{15.9} \end{equation*} Now assume that $X \supset g^{+}(k)+g^{-}(k)$ for some $k$. Then $Y$ contains no genes of rank $k$. Since $X<Y$, we have $Y^{(k)} 
eq 0$. By (15.9) we have $a_{k}<c_{k}$ or $b_{k}<d_{k}$. We define a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g^{+}(k)+g^{-}(k) ightarrow g^{\varepsilon}(k+1)+g^{-\varepsilon}(k-1)$, where\ $\varepsilon=+$ if $a_{k}<c_{k}$ and $\varepsilon=-$ otherwise. Clearly $Z \leqslant Y$. Hence we may assume from now on (and we do) that  egin{equation*} X 
ot \supset g^{+}(k)+g^{-}(k), \quad k \geqslant 1 . 	ag{15.10} \end{equation*} Until further notice we shall assume that $a_{1}<c_{1}$. Let $g_{1}=g_{\varepsilon_{1}}(m)$ be a gene of $X$ with $m$ minimal. Case 1: $\varepsilon_{1}=-$. Since $a_{1}<c_{1}, X$ contains a gene of the form $g_{2}=g_{+}(k)$. Assume also that $k$ is minimal. We define a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g_{1}+g_{2} ightarrow$ $g_{-}(k+1)+g_{+}(m-1)$. If $k$ is odd we have $$  egin{aligned} c_{k-1}-c_{k} & \leqslant d_{k-2}-d_{k-1} \leqslant \cdots \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1}<a_{0}-a_{1} \ & =b_{1}-b_{2}=\cdots=b_{k-2}-b_{k-1}=a_{k-1}-a_{k} . \end{aligned} $$ Therefore $a_{k}<c_{k}, b_{k-1}<d_{k-1}, a_{k-2}<c_{k-2}, \ldots, b_{2}<d_{2}$ and so $Z \leqslant Y$. If $k$ is even we have $d_{k-1}-d_{k} \leqslant c_{k-2}-c_{k-1} \leqslant \cdots \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1}<a_{0}-a_{1}=b_{1}-b_{2}=\cdots$ $=a_{k-2}-a_{k-1}=b_{k-1}-b_{k}$. Hence $b_{k}<d_{k}, a_{k-1}<c_{k-1}, b_{k-2}<d_{k-2}, \ldots, b_{2}<$ $d_{2}$, and again we have $Z \leqslant Y$. Case 2: $g_{1}=g_{+}(1)$. Since $b_{0}=c_{0} \geqslant c_{1}>a_{1}, X$ must contain a negative gene. Let $g_{2}=g^{-}(k)$ be such a gene with $k$ minimal. Define a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g_{1}+g_{2} ightarrow g^{+}(k+1)$. For $1 \leqslant i \leqslant k$ we have $$ d_{0}-d_{i-1} \geqslant c_{1}-c_{i}>a_{1}-c_{i}=\left(a_{1}-a_{i}ight)+\left(a_{i}-c_{i}ight)=b_{0}-b_{i-1}+\left(a_{i}-c_{i}ight) . $$ Since $b_{0}=d_{0}$ and $b_{i-1} \leqslant d_{i-1}$, we infer that $a_{i}<c_{i}$, and so $Z \leqslant Y$.\ Case 3: $\varepsilon_{1}=+, m \geqslant 2$, and $X \supset 2 g_{1}$. We define the $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $2 g_{1} ightarrow g_{+}(m-2)+g_{+}(m+2)$. We have $b_{1}-b_{2}=a_{0}-a_{1}>c_{0}-c_{1} \geqslant d_{1}-d_{2}$, and so $b_{2}<d_{2}$. Let $m$ be even and $i=m-1$ or $m$. Then $c_{1}-c_{i} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{i-1} \leqslant b_{0}$ $-b_{i-1}=a_{1}-a_{i}$ and $d_{2}-d_{i+1} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{i-1} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{i-1}=b_{2}-b_{i+1}$. Consequently, $a_{i}<c_{i}$ and $b_{i+1}<d_{i+1}$, and so $Z \leqslant Y$. Now let $m$ be odd and $i=m-1$ or $m$. Then $c_{1}-c_{i+1} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{i} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{i}=a_{1}-a_{i+1}$ and $d_{2}-d_{i} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{i-2} \leqslant b_{0}$ - $b_{i-2}=b_{2}-b_{i}$. Since $a_{1}<c_{1}$ and $b_{2}<d_{2}$, it follows that $a_{i+1}<c_{i+1}$ and $b_{i}<d_{i}$. Thus we have again $Z \leqslant Y$. Case 4: $\varepsilon_{1}=+, m \geqslant 2$, and $X 
ot \supset 2 g_{1}$. Clearly $X^{(m)} 
eq 0$, and let $g_{2}=g^{\varepsilon_{2}}(k)$ be a gene of $X$ with $k$ minimal subject to $k>m$. Note that $g_{1}=g^{\varepsilon}(m)$ where $\varepsilon=(-1)^{m-1}$. Let first $\varepsilon_{2}=-\varepsilon$. Then we define a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g_{1}+g_{2} ightarrow g^{\varepsilon}(k+1)+$ $g^{-\varepsilon}(m-1)$. As in Case 3 we have $a_{m}<c_{m}$ and $b_{m}<d_{m}$. Let $m$ be even and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-1$. For $i$ even we have $d_{i}-d_{i+1} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{1} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{1}=b_{i}-b_{i+1}$, and for $i$ odd $$  egin{aligned} d_{i}-d_{i+1} & \leqslant d_{1}-d_{2} \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1} \leqslant a_{0}-a_{1}-1 \ & =b_{m-1}-b_{m}-1=a_{m}-a_{m+1}=b_{i}-b_{i+1} . \end{aligned} $$ Since $b_{m}<d_{m}$, we obtain $b_{i}<d_{i}(m \leqslant i \leqslant k)$, and so $Z \leqslant Y$. Now let $m$ be odd and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-1$. For $i$ even we have $$ c_{i}-c_{i+1} \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1} \leqslant a_{0}-a_{1}-1=a_{m-1}-a_{m}-1=b_{m}-b_{m+1}=a_{i}-a_{i+1}, $$ and for $i$ odd $c_{i}-c_{i+1} \leqslant c_{1}-c_{2} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{1} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{1}=a_{i}-a_{i+1}$. Since $a_{m}<c_{m}$, we obtain $a_{i}<c_{i}(m \leqslant i \leqslant k)$, and so we have again $Z \leqslant Y$. Now let $\varepsilon_{2}=\varepsilon$ and let $k-m$ be even. Then we define a $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g_{1}+g_{2} ightarrow g^{\varepsilon}(k+2)+g^{\varepsilon}(m-2)$. As in Case 3 we have $a_{m-1}<c_{m-1}, a_{m}<c_{m}$, $b_{m}<d_{m}$ and, if $m>2, b_{m-1}<d_{m-1}$. Let $m$ be even and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-1$. For $i$ even we have $$ c_{i}-c_{i+1} \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1} \leqslant a_{0}-a_{1}-1=a_{m}-a_{m+1}=a_{i}-a_{i+1}, $$ and for $i$ odd $c_{i}-c_{i+1} \leqslant c_{1}-c_{2} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{1} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{1}=a_{i}-a_{i+1}$. It follows that $a_{i}<c_{i}$ for $m-1 \leqslant i \leqslant k$. The proof of $b_{i}<d_{i}(m \leqslant i \leqslant k+1)$ is the same as when $\varepsilon_{2}=-\varepsilon$ except that one has to use also the equality $a_{k-1}-a_{k}=b_{k}-b_{k+1}$. Thus $Z \leqslant Y$. Now let $m$ be odd and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-1$. For $i$ even we have $d_{i}-d_{i+1} \leqslant d_{0}-d_{1} \leqslant b_{0}-b_{1}=b_{i}-b_{i+1}$, and for $i$ odd $$ d_{i}-d_{i+1} \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1} \leqslant a_{0}-a_{1}-1=b_{m}-b_{m+1}=b_{i}-b_{i+1} . $$ Thus $b_{i}<d_{i}$ for $m-1 \leqslant i \leqslant k$. The proof of $a_{i}<c_{i}(m \leqslant i \leqslant k+1)$ is the same as when $\varepsilon_{2}=-\varepsilon$ except that one has to use also the equality $b_{k-1}-b_{k}=a_{k}-a_{k+1}$. Hence again $Z \leqslant Y$. Finally let $\varepsilon_{2}=\varepsilon$ and let $k-m$ be odd. Then we define the $\Pi$-mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g_{1}+g_{2} ightarrow g^{\varepsilon}(k+1)+g^{\varepsilon}(m-1)$. The proof of $Z \leqslant Y$ is the same as when $k-m$ is even. This completes the Case 4 and exhausts all possibilities when $a_{1}<c_{1}$. If $b_{1}<d_{1}$ then by interchanging positive and negative charges in both $X$ and $Y$, we reduce this case to the case $a_{1}<c_{1}$. In view of (15.9) this completes the proof.\ 16. The cases $ oldsymbol{\Phi}=(\Pi, \Lambda)$ or $(\Lambda, \Pi)$. We shall treat the two cases simultaneously. All mutations in this proof are understood to be $\Phi$-mutations. Let $X, Y \in \Phi(n)$ and $X<Y$. We shall show that there exists a mutation $X ightarrow Z$ such that $Z \leqslant Y$. The proof is by induction on $n$. For $n=0,1$ the assertion is vacuously true, so let $n>1$. We shall write sig $X^{(i)}=\left(a_{i}, b_{i}ight)$, sign $Y^{(i)}=\left(c_{i}, d_{i}ight), r_{i}=a_{i}+b_{i}, s_{i}=c_{i}+d_{i}$. Since $X<Y$, we have $\left(a_{i}, b_{i}ight) \leqslant\left(c_{i}, d_{i}ight)$ for all $i$. Since $a_{0}+b_{0}=n=c_{0}+d_{0}$, we have $a_{0}=c_{0}$ and $b_{0}=d_{0}$. If $\Phi=(\Lambda, \Pi)$ (resp., $\Phi=(\Pi, \Lambda)$ ) then $a_{i}=b_{i}$ and $c_{i}=d_{i}$ for $i$ odd (resp., $i$ even), and $a_{i}, b_{i}, c_{i}, d_{i}$ are integers for $i$ even (resp. $i$ odd). As in the previous section we may assume that $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint. In view of the lifting property and our induction hypothesis, we may assume (and we do) that  egin{equation*} Y^{(i)} 
eq 0 \& i \geqslant 1 \Rightarrow r_{i}<s_{i} . 	ag{16.1} \end{equation*} Assume that $X \supset g^{+}(m)+g^{-}(m)$ for some $m$. We define the mutation $X ightarrow Z$ by $g^{+}(m)+g^{-}(m) ightarrow g(m-1)+g(m+1)$. Since $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint, we have $Y^{(m)} 
eq 0$. Then (16.1) implies that $r_{m}<s_{m}$, and consequently $Z \leqslant Y$. Hence from now on we may assume that $X 
ot \supset g^{+}(m)+g^{-}(m), m \geqslant 1$. Next assume that $X \supset 2 g(m)$ for some $m$. Since $X$ and $Y$ are disjoint and $X<Y$, we have $Y^{(m)} 
eq 0$. By (16.1) we have $a_{m}<c_{m}$ or $b_{m}<d_{m}$. Then we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$ where the mutation $X ightarrow Z$ is defined by $2 g(m) ightarrow g^{-\varepsilon}(m-1)+$ $g^{\varepsilon}(m+1) ; \varepsilon=+$ if $a_{m}<c_{m}$ and $\varepsilon=-$ if $a_{m}=c_{m}$. Without loss of generality we assume from now on that $X 
ot \supset 2 g(m), m \geqslant 1$. From now on let $g_{1}$ be a gene of $X$ of minimum rank $r\left(g_{1}ight)=m$. When $g_{1}$ is nonpolarized we denote by $g_{2}$ a gene of $X-g_{1}$ of minimum rank $r\left(g_{2}ight)=k$. Since $g_{1}=g(m)$ and $X 
ot \supset 2 g(m)$, we have $m<k$. Then we distinguish two cases. Case 1: $g_{2}=g(k)$. By (16.1) $r_{m}<s_{m}$, and so $a_{m}<c_{m}$ or $b_{m}<d_{m}$. By switching + and - charges in all polarized genes of $X$ and $Y$ (if necessary) we may assume that $a_{m}<c_{m}$. If $i-m$ is even and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-2$ then $$ c_{i}-c_{i+2} \leqslant s_{i}-s_{i+1} \leqslant s_{0}-s_{1} \leqslant r_{0}-r_{1}-1=r_{i}-r_{i+1}=a_{i}-a_{i+2} . $$ Since $a_{m}<c_{m}$, these inequalities imply that $a_{j}<c_{j}$ for $m \leqslant j \leqslant k$. Hence we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$, where $X ightarrow Z$ is defined by $g(m)+g(k) ightarrow g^{-}(m-1)+g^{+}(k+1)$. Case 2: $g_{2}$ is polarized. Without loss of generality we may assume that $g_{2}=g^{+}(k)$. If $i-m$ is even and $m \leqslant i \leqslant k-3$ then $d_{i}-d_{i+2} \leqslant s_{i}-s_{i+1} \leqslant s_{0}-s_{1} \leqslant r_{0}-r_{1}$ $-1=r_{i}-r_{i+1}=b_{i}-b_{i+2}$. If $m \geqslant 2$ we have $$ d_{m-2}-d_{m} \leqslant s_{m-2}-s_{m-1} \leqslant s_{0}-s_{1}<r_{0}-r_{1}=r_{m-2}-r_{m-1}=b_{m-2}-b_{m-1}, $$ and so $b_{m}<d_{m}$. Thus if $m \geqslant 2$ or if $m=1$ and $b_{1}<d_{1}$, it follows that $b_{j}<d_{j}$ for $m \leqslant j \leqslant k-1$. Under these conditions we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$, where $X ightarrow Z$ is defined by $g(m)+g^{+}(k) ightarrow g^{+}(m-1)+g(k+1)$. Now let $m=1$ and $b_{1}=d_{1}$. Then $c_{1}-a_{1}=s_{1}-r_{1} \geqslant 1$ and consequently $X-g_{1}-g_{2}$ must contain either a negative or a nonpolarized gene. Let $g_{3}$ be such a gene of minimal rank $r\left(g_{3}ight)=t$. If $i$ is even and $2 \leqslant i \leqslant t-1$ then $$ c_{i}-c_{i+1} \leqslant c_{0}-c_{1} \leqslant a_{0}-a_{1}-1<b_{1}-b_{2}=a_{2}-a_{3}=\cdots=a_{i}-a_{i+1} . $$ Hence $a_{j}<c_{j}$ for $1 \leqslant j \leqslant t$. If $t$ is even then $g_{3}=g^{-}(t)$ and we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$, with $X ightarrow Z$ defined by $g_{2}+g_{3} ightarrow g(k-1)+g(t+1)$. If $t$ is odd then $g_{3}=g(t)$ and we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$, with $X ightarrow Z$ defined by $g_{2}+g_{3} ightarrow g(k-1)+g^{+}(t+1)$. Now assume that $g_{1}$ is polarized. Without any loss of generality we assume that $g_{1}=g^{+}(m)$. We now distinguish several cases. Case 3: $m \geqslant 3$. We have $$  egin{aligned} & c_{m-1}-c_{m+1} \leqslant s_{m-1}-s_{m} \leqslant s_{0}-s_{1}<r_{0}-r_{1}=a_{m-1}-a_{m+1}, \ & d_{m-3}-d_{m-1} \leqslant s_{m-3}-s_{m-2} \leqslant s_{0}-s_{1}<r_{0}-r_{1}=b_{m-3}-b_{m-1}, \end{aligned} $$ and so $a_{m+1}<c_{m+1}$ and $b_{m-1}<d_{m-1}$. If $X \supset 2 g_{1}$ then we have $X ightarrow Z \leqslant Y$, where $X ightarrow Z$ is defined by $2 g_{1} ightarrow g^{+}(m-2)+g^{+}(m+2)$. We may now assume that $X 
